{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Protecting Indigenous peoples’ rights, cultures, languages, and political voice is a legitimate policy goal. But preserving a fixed population share through immigration limits is a blunt approach: demographic proportions alone don’t ensure land rights, self-determination, or meaningful influence.\n\nA stronger policy would be developed with Indigenous peoples and assessed by whether it protects their rights and strengthens their communities. Immigration’s effects on housing, land, and public services can be part of that assessment, without making ancestry the sole criterion for admission."}}
